🚽 Test case: regression for #206 (3a) code review -- StructField dropped
🚽 modifiers entirely (no field to hold them), so a field reached through
🚽 a `lit giga` alias silently sized/aligned as a plain 4-byte int instead
🚽 of an 8-byte long. get_struct_field_size()/get_struct_field_alignment()
🚽 (ast.c) now both read f->modifiers -- this is an LP64 claim, not a
🚽 platform-independent one: `giga` (long) is 4 bytes on wasm32's ILP32
🚽 (see tests/run_wasm_tests.mjs's override for this fixture); `thicc`
🚽 (long long) would be 8 on both, untested here.
🚽
🚽 A second regression, caught in the same review round: create_struct_
🚽 access_node()/its semantic_analyzer.c counterpart copied fld->type and
🚽 fld->pointer_level onto the NODE_STRUCT_ACCESS node but not fld->
🚽 modifiers, so `maxxing(d.value)` alone (member access, not the whole
🚽 struct) still reported 4 even after the fix above -- the member's own
🚽 sizeof disagreed with the slot the outer struct had just reserved for
🚽 it. Both now copy modifiers too.
🚽
🚽 What this test does NOT prove: that `d.value` is stored as a real
🚽 8-byte C `long`. It isn't -- evaluate_expression_int()/write_value_to_
🚽 address() still load/store every VAR_INT through a 4-byte `int`,
🚽 struct field or not. That is a pre-existing gap in `giga`/`thicc`
🚽 outside of structs too (a bare top-level `giga rizz x = 5000000000;`
🚽 already truncates the same way), not something introduced or fixed by
🚽 this PR. This test only proves the *slot* -- its size and its member's
🚽 own reported sizeof -- is C-correct; it deliberately does not assign a
🚽 value outside int range to `d.value`, because that would demonstrate
🚽 the still-open truncation, not this fix.
lit giga rizz Meters;

gang Distance {
    yap unit;    🚽 char,        1 byte, align 1 -> offset 0
    Meters value; 🚽 long (giga), 8 bytes, align 8 -> offset 8 (7 bytes padding)
};              🚽 total: 16, not 5 (1 + 4, if Meters were misread as int)

skibidi main {
    gang Distance d;
    yapping("%lu", maxxing(d));
    yapping("%lu", maxxing(d.value));
}
